<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teatro all'Antica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1588">1588</a>–<a href="/years/1590">1590</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Teatro Olimpico of Sabbioneta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teatro all'Antica is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in Sabbioneta, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincenzo-scamozzi">Vincenzo Scamozzi</a> for Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vespasiano-gonzaga">Vespasiano Gonzaga</a> and built in 1588–1590. Inspired by ancient theatres and by Palladio's Teatro Olimpico, it was one of the first free-standing buildings in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> erected specifically as a theatre. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> of Mantua and Sabbioneta.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teatro-allantica.jpg" alt="Teatro all'Antica" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ugo franchini · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_Olimpico_Sabbioneta_-_Galleria_o_PERGULA_-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in the style of the ancients. Duke Vespasiano I Gonzaga commissioned it as part of his effort to turn his small ducal seat into an idealised classical city. Its place on the town's principal street, the Via Giulia, and its separate building show the standing theatre had come to hold as a mark of the civilised society the duke was trying to create. The prestigious site was cramped and narrow, however, and its conversion into a theatre demanded considerable ingenuity from Scamozzi. It is the second-oldest surviving indoor theatre in the world, after the Teatro Olimpico in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a>, and with that theatre and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teatro-farnese">Teatro Farnese</a> in Parma one of only three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> theatres still standing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Scamozzi had overseen the completion of the Teatro Olimpico after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio">Andrea Palladio</a>, and had designed the perspective scenery on its stage. Its influence shows in the colonnade behind the seating and in the set. The building at Sabbioneta, however, is about three times as long as it is wide, where the Olimpico's space is nearly square. Palladio had been forced to stretch the ideal semicircle of a Roman theatre into an ellipse; Scamozzi was forced the other way, and his seating became a horseshoe.</p><p>Scamozzi also gave up the elaborate classical scaenae frons that defines the Olimpico and had obliged him to build seven separate street scenes there. Instead he built a single trompe-l'œil view of one street. The false-front buildings shrink rapidly over a short distance, the floor rises steeply and the two sides of the street close in, giving the illusion of great depth. With so narrow an auditorium, one perspective served every seat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 17th century the original scenery was removed and replaced by a system of sliding wings. Scamozzi's plans, which show the perspective street in their upper right corner, were kept in the archives of the Uffizi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and in the twentieth century new scenery was built from them and set in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teatro all'Antica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sabbioneta was laid out on a regular plan with star-shaped walls, a ducal palace, a long gallery for Vespasiano's art and antiquities, and the <b>Teatro all'Antica</b> by Vincenzo Scamozzi, completed around 1590, one of the first purpose-built theatres in modern Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-sabbioneta">Duchy of Sabbioneta</a></li></ul></section>
